# Production (and staging): Docker Compose's variables. Copy it, then fill it:
#
#   cp env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Keep .env out of git (it is ignored). Every other variable, with its
# default: docs/configuration.md and compose.prod.yaml.
#
# REQUIRED: APP_DOMAIN, ACME_EMAIL, CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD (the last three, or their files: "Secrets in files"
# at the end).
#
# Generate each secret on its own (never reuse one value for two names):
#   passwords (they go into URLs: hex only)   openssl rand -hex 24
#   keys and signing secrets                  openssl rand -base64 32

# Docker Compose: the project (names containers and volumes), its files, how
# many app instances, and the deployment's name (staging: same stack, its own
# .env, APP_ENV=staging). With COMPOSE_FILE set, `docker compose up -d --build`
# needs no -f flags.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=nestjs-monorepo-template
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml
API_REPLICAS=2
WORKER_REPLICAS=1
APP_ENV=production
# Optional: run an image CI published instead of building one here
# (docs/deployment.md, "Publishing and rolling back images"). Then
# `docker compose pull migrate api worker && docker compose up -d`, without --build.
# APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>:<full commit SHA>

# Caddy: HTTPS for APP_DOMAIN, through a Cloudflare DNS challenge. REQUIRED.
# APP_DOMAIN: replace the placeholder with the API's host name.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: a Cloudflare token with Zone.DNS edit on the domain's
# zone. Moving from an earlier deployment? Copy the token and email from its
# Caddy container:
#   docker inspect --format '{{json .Config.Env}}' <caddy container>
APP_DOMAIN=api.example.com
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
ACME_EMAIL=

# PostgreSQL of the stack: two roles (docs/deployment.md, "Database roles").
# POSTGRES_USER (default app) owns the tables and runs the migrations and the
# backups; POSTGRES_APP_USER (default app_runtime) is what api and worker
# connect as: it owns nothing, can't change the schema and can't bypass
# row-level security. `migrate` creates it on the first deploy from these
# values. Both passwords are REQUIRED (hex: they go into URLs), or their
# files (DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE and DATABASE_URL_FILE, at the end).
#   openssl rand -hex 24      (one each)
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=
# Managed PostgreSQL instead: DATABASE_URL (the app's role) and
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL (the owner: your admin account, with CREATEROLE) replace
# the four above; leave postgres and backup out of the stack.
# DATABASE_URL=
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL=
# Redis: optional but recommended (unset, Redis runs without a password).
#   openssl rand -hex 24
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# Backups (docs/deployment.md, "Backups and restore"): the database, and the
# uploaded files with STORAGE_DRIVER=local, on a cron schedule (UTC), kept
# this many days in the backups volume. Unhealthy when the newest successful
# backup is older than BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS (raise it with a rarer schedule).
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=0 3 * * *
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=25
# Off the host, recommended (empty bucket: local copies only, lost with the
# disk). A bucket of its own and keys for it alone, not the app's S3_*; give
# the bucket a lifecycle rule, since the backup never deletes there.
# R2: endpoint https://<account id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, region auto.
# AWS: no endpoint; empty keys use the host's IAM role.
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_REGION=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Container logs: per container, at most DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE files of
# DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE, compressed. local is the driver Docker recommends;
# json-file only for a log shipper that reads Docker's log files.
DOCKER_LOG_DRIVER=local
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE=20m
DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE=5

# The web app (links in emails, CORS) and the time zone of cron jobs and emails.
WEB_URL=
CORS_ORIGINS=
APP_TIMEZONE=UTC

# Notifications: the hour (0-23, in each user's own time zone) daily email
# digests are sent; how many days read notifications are kept, and any at all.
NOTIFICATIONS_DIGEST_HOUR=8
NOTIFICATIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=90
NOTIFICATIONS_MAX_AGE_DAYS=365

# Bulk operations (POST /v1/<x>/bulk): up to this many ids run in the
# request; more, and every filter selection, run on the workers.
BULK_SYNC_LIMIT=100

# How many days a user has to cancel the deletion of their account.
ACCOUNT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=14

# API clients' usage per day and route (GET /v1/usage): kept this many days.
USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=400

# Record revisions (GET /v1/notes/{id}/revisions): kept this many days; each
# record's latest revision always stays.
REVISIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# Exchange rates (docs/money.md): the workers fetch the ECB's euro reference
# rates every working day (off: an app without money). The URL is the
# directory of the ECB's eurofxref documents, or a mirror of it.
EXCHANGE_RATES_FETCH=on
EXCHANGE_RATES_ECB_URL=https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/eurofxref

# Scheduled reports (POST /v1/reports): how long the mailed download link
# works (30m, 24h, 3d; at most 7d). Anyone holding the link (a forwarded
# mail) can download the file until then.
REPORT_LINK_TTL=24h

# Files attached to records (POST /v1/<x>/{id}/attachments): the largest, in
# MB, and the types allowed (detected from the bytes; comma-separated MIME
# types). Empty: images, PDF, Office (docx, xlsx, pptx), text, csv, zip.
ATTACHMENT_MAX_MB=25
ATTACHMENT_ALLOWED_TYPES=

# Mail: log until you set a provider (smtp + SMTP_URL, or
# resend/postmark/sendgrid + MAIL_API_KEY, or ses).
MAIL_TRANSPORT=log
MAIL_FROM=
SMTP_URL=
MAIL_API_KEY=

# Deliverability (docs/deliverability.md): the SNS topic(s) SES publishes
# bounces and complaints to (comma-separated ARNs; empty: the webhook
# refuses everything), subscribed to https://<APP_DOMAIN>/v1/webhooks/mail/ses.
# Addresses that bounce hard or complain stop getting mail; one that bounces
# softly THRESHOLD times (each within WINDOW_HOURS of the last) stops for
# SUPPRESSION_HOURS. Events are kept RETENTION_DAYS. Once every topic has
# SignatureVersion 2, REQUIRE_SIGNATURE_V2=on refuses version 1 (SHA1).
SES_SNS_TOPIC_ARNS=
SES_SNS_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE_V2=off
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_THRESHOLD=3
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_WINDOW_HOURS=24
MAIL_SOFT_BOUNCE_SUPPRESSION_HOURS=72
MAIL_EVENTS_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# The first administrator, created on the first start. ADMIN_PASSWORD empty:
# a password is generated and logged once (`docker compose logs api`). Change
# it after signing in.
#   openssl rand -base64 18
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# New passwords found in data breaches are refused (Have I Been Pwned's
# Pwned Passwords, k-anonymity: only 5 characters of the password's SHA-1
# are sent). On by default. The API URL can be a self-hosted mirror; when it
# can't be reached in PASSWORD_BREACH_TIMEOUT_MS, the password is allowed
# with a warning (allow) or refused with a 503 (reject). A password seen
# fewer than PASSWORD_BREACH_MIN_COUNT times passes. docs/auth.md, Passwords.
PASSWORD_BREACH_CHECK=on
PASSWORD_BREACH_API_URL=https://api.pwnedpasswords.com
PASSWORD_BREACH_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
PASSWORD_BREACH_ON_ERROR=allow
PASSWORD_BREACH_MIN_COUNT=1

# Password hashing (scrypt: 128 MiB outside the heap and ~0.4 s of CPU each):
# at most this many at once per api process (the worker: 1), the next ones
# wait in order, and past PASSWORD_HASH_MAX_QUEUE waiting or
# PASSWORD_HASH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS of waiting they get 503 server_busy. Raising
# the concurrency needs API_MEMORY raised with it (128 MiB each):
# docs/deployment.md, "Resource limits".
API_PASSWORD_HASH_CONCURRENCY=2
WORKER_PASSWORD_HASH_CONCURRENCY=1
PASSWORD_HASH_MAX_QUEUE=20
PASSWORD_HASH_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Bot protection on sign-in (browser and token), sign-up, the magic-link
# request and forgot password (docs/auth.md, "Bot protection"): a Cloudflare
# Turnstile token in X-Captcha-Token, always, or after CAPTCHA_FAILURES
# failed attempts from an address or for an account within a fixed window of
# CAPTCHA_FAILURES_WINDOW_MINUTES from the first one (after-failures). Off by default; the mode on needs both Turnstile keys
# (the dashboard's widget: TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY is public, the secret key can
# be TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY_FILE below). The provider unreachable in
# CAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS: allow the request under CAPTCHA_DEGRADED_LIMIT per
# address in the window (fail-open, with a metric and an alert), or reject
# it with a 503. CAPTCHA_HOSTNAMES: the web app's hosts the widget runs on
# (default: WEB_URL's). Never CAPTCHA_PROVIDER=stub here: production refuses it.
CAPTCHA_MODE=off
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
CAPTCHA_FAILURES=3
CAPTCHA_FAILURES_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
CAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
CAPTCHA_ON_ERROR=allow
CAPTCHA_DEGRADED_LIMIT=2
CAPTCHA_HOSTNAMES=

# Keys: comma-separated, newest first (add a new one in front to rotate).
# Empty, the app creates IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS, MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS,
# STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS and JWT_SECRET on the first start and keeps them in the
# database (table app_secrets). Set them here to keep them out of the database
# and its backups, or to rotate them.
# WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS is never created: empty, webhook endpoint secrets
# are stored unencrypted. Set it.
#   openssl rand -base64 32      (one per name)
IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS=
WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS=
JWT_SECRET=

# NestJS Observe (traces, metrics, errors), off when empty: the application's
# keys from its project's API keys page.
OBSERVE_APP_KEY=
OBSERVE_APP_SECRET=

# Prometheus metrics at GET /metrics, off (404) when empty: the bearer token
# Prometheus sends (at least 16 characters). Scraped inside the Compose
# network only: Caddy answers /metrics with 404 (docs/deployment.md, Metrics).
#   openssl rand -hex 24
METRICS_TOKEN=

# Not for a deployment: RATE_LIMIT_MULTIPLIER (every rate limit times it,
# default 1) is set by compose.loadtest.yaml for the load test's throwaway
# stack only (docs/development.md, "Load tests"); compose.prod.yaml doesn't
# pass it to the app, and in production the app refuses it above 1 unless
# APP_ENV=loadtest.
# RATE_LIMIT_MULTIPLIER=1

# Alerting (optional): Prometheus, Alertmanager and the PostgreSQL and Redis
# exporters, once compose.monitoring.yaml is added to COMPOSE_FILE above
# (COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.prod.yaml:compose.monitoring.yaml). It
# needs METRICS_TOKEN. Alerts go to any of these; none set, they only show in
# the UIs (docs/deployment.md, Alerting).
# ALERT_EMAIL_TO: comma-separated addresses. The mail goes through
# ALERT_SMTP_URL (empty: SMTP_URL above; smtp:// with STARTTLS or smtps://,
# user and percent-encoded password in it), from ALERT_EMAIL_FROM (empty:
# MAIL_FROM). ALERT_SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=false only for a relay without STARTTLS.
ALERT_EMAIL_TO=
ALERT_SMTP_URL=
ALERT_EMAIL_FROM=
ALERT_SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=true
# A generic webhook (Alertmanager's JSON) and a Slack incoming webhook.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
# The dead man's switch, off when both are empty: the always-firing Watchdog
# alert pings this URL about once a minute (a healthchecks.io check: period
# 1 minute, grace 5 minutes), and that check alarms when the pings stop.
# Preferred: an absolute path to a file outside the repository holding the
# URL, mounted as a Docker secret (readable by the container: chmod 644 in a
# chmod 700 directory). Or the URL itself (the file wins).
ALERT_WATCHDOG_URL_FILE=
ALERT_WATCHDOG_URL=
# The HTTPS probe's host name (TLS server name and Host header for Caddy);
# empty: APP_DOMAIN.
PROBE_HOST=
# The UIs listen on the host's 127.0.0.1 only (reach them through an SSH
# tunnel), and the containers' limits.
PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
ALERTMANAGER_PORT=9093
PROMETHEUS_CPUS=0.5
PROMETHEUS_MEMORY=1G
ALERTMANAGER_CPUS=0.25
ALERTMANAGER_MEMORY=128M

# The example feature: its incoming webhook sender's secret (empty: created on
# the first start, like the keys above; give it to the sender), and how long a
# note waits before it is published.
#   echo "whsec_$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
EXAMPLE_SENDER_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
NOTE_PUBLISH_DELAY=10s

# Secrets in files (optional; docs/deployment.md, "Secrets in files"): any
# secret above can live in a file instead, mounted as a Docker secret, so its
# value is in no variable. X_FILE: the file's absolute path, outside the
# repository, readable by the containers (chmod 644 in a chmod 700
# directory); then empty X itself (both set: the service refuses to start).
# A changed file needs `docker compose up -d --force-recreate`. The database
# takes three: POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE (PostgreSQL, backups, the exporter; LF
# line endings, PostgreSQL's image keeps a CR), DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE (the
# owner's URL with that password, postgres://app:<password>@postgres:5432/app,
# mounted on migrate only) and DATABASE_URL_FILE (the app role's URL with
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD's value,
# postgres://app_runtime:<password>@postgres:5432/app; api, worker, migrate).
# POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD has no file of its own: the whole URL is the secret.
# METRICS_TOKEN stays a variable with compose.monitoring.yaml.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=
DATABASE_OWNER_URL_FILE=
DATABASE_URL_FILE=
REDIS_PASSWORD_FILE=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN_FILE=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=
JWT_SECRET_FILE=
MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_FILE=
STORAGE_SIGNING_KEYS_FILE=
IDEMPOTENCY_KEYS_FILE=
WEBHOOKS_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_FILE=
SMTP_URL_FILE=
MAIL_API_KEY_FILE=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY_FILE=
OBSERVE_APP_SECRET_FILE=
EXAMPLE_SENDER_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=
